## 9. Debugging and Lessons Learned

### Symptom

The `ADD_4bit` block produced incorrect results after it was integrated into the complete ALU. Some individual tests appeared correct, but other input combinations failed.

### Root Cause

The problem was inside the 1-bit Full Adder subcircuit. Its carry logic was incomplete or connected incorrectly.

Because `ADD_4bit` uses four Full Adders, an error in this sub-block affected the carry chain and caused the final ALU result to be incorrect.

### How the Bug Was Found

I traced the result backward through the design hierarchy:

```text
Top-level ALU
    -> ADD_4bit
        -> Full Adder 1-bit
```

Testing the smaller blocks separately helped identify the Full Adder as the source of the error.

### Fix

I corrected the Full Adder carry logic and checked the Ripple Carry connections:

```text
FA0.Cout -> FA1.Cin
FA1.Cout -> FA2.Cin
FA2.Cout -> FA3.Cin
```

### Regression Test

After the fix, I repeated the addition tests, including cases that generate a carry:

| A | B | SUM | Cout |
|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| `1001` | `1001` | `0010` | `1` |
| `1111` | `0001` | `0000` | `1` |
| `1111` | `1111` | `1110` | `1` |

This debugging process showed me why testing both individual subcircuits and the complete integrated design is important.

